<h2 id="s1">1 · The concept: one repo, two engines</h2>

<p>This repo is deliberately built as <strong>two layers of a trading firm in miniature</strong>.
Real quant trading firms split work exactly this way:</p>

<div class="arch">
  <div class="archbox py">
    <h4>🧠 Research layer — Python (<code>src/oil_alpha/</code>)</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem; margin:.3rem 0;">Answers <em>“is there a tradeable signal?”</em>
    Runs at <strong>daily</strong> frequency. Speed doesn't matter; statistical correctness does.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Downloads CL=F / XLE / USO / XOM / CVX closes (yfinance, FRED, Stooq)</li>
      <li>Cleans known data traps (Apr-2020 negative WTI, roll days)</li>
      <li>Lead-lag battery: CCF, Granger causality, cointegration</li>
      <li>Costs the hypothetical trade in basis points</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="archbox cpp">
    <h4>⚡ Execution-style layer — C++ (<code>cpp/</code>)</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem; margin:.3rem 0;">Answers <em>“can we process the market fast enough?”</em>
    Runs at <strong>nanosecond</strong> scale. This is the genuinely HFT part.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>ITCH 5.0 binary feed parser (Add / Execute / Cancel / Delete / Replace)</li>
      <li>Two limit-order-book implementations: <code>std::map</code> baseline vs flat array</li>
      <li>Top-of-book signal (best bid / ask / mid)</li>
      <li>HDR-style latency histogram: p50 / p99 / p99.9 in nanoseconds</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="bridge">🔗 Bridge: <strong>pybind11</strong> (<code>cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</code> → <code>oil_volume_cpp</code>) —
  covered in <a href="0001-pybind11-cmake-extension.html">Lesson 0001</a></div>
</div>

<p>Hold onto this framing: <strong>the Python layer finds the alpha; the C++ layer is practice
for the machinery that would trade it at high frequency.</strong> The README's "out of scope"
list (Kyle's&nbsp;λ, OFI, microprice, Avellaneda-Stoikov) is the future work that will connect the two.</p>

