<h2 id="s4">4 · In this project: where each piece lives</h2>

<h3>The pipeline spine</h3>
<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/pipeline.py</span> — <code>run_pipeline()</code> walks six stages:
<code>download → spot-check → clean → lead-lag → cointegration → costs</code>. Each stage is wrapped in a
<code>StageTimer</code> (from <span class="path">src/oil_alpha/metrics.py</span>) so per-stage compute time
lands on the dashboard. Note the defensive pattern at each stage: failures append to
<code>result.warnings</code> and the pipeline degrades instead of crashing.</p>

<h3>The statistics</h3>
<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/analysis/leadlag.py</span> — <code>ccf_leadlag()</code> computes
<code>ρ(k) = corr(x_t, y_{t+k})</code> <em>by hand</em> (mean-removed dot products) rather than trusting a
library's lag convention. A peak at <strong>k &gt; 0 means x leads y</strong>. The docstring states this
explicitly and a unit test pins it against a synthetic known lead — a convention that "can't silently
drift." <code>granger_both_directions()</code> runs the test both ways because predictive precedence ≠ causation.</p>

<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/config.py</span> — the study's protagonists
(<code>LEAD = "wti_fut"</code>, <code>LAG = "energy_etf"</code>), the Apr-2020 negative-price window
constants, free-tier API caps, and the basis-point cost model
(<code>AssetCost.round_trip_bp</code> = cross the spread twice + commission twice).</p>

<h3>The HFT engine</h3>
<p><span class="path">cpp/itch/itch_parser.hpp</span> — a subset of Nasdaq TotalView-ITCH 5.0:
message types <code>'A' 'F' 'E' 'C' 'X' 'D' 'U'</code>, all fields big-endian, prices fixed-point
(× 10000), each message preceded by a 2-byte length (MoldUDP64-style framing). The parser
<em>never reads past the buffer</em> and counts skipped/error messages — feed handlers must be
paranoid because a malformed message must not take down the trading system.</p>

<p><span class="path">cpp/itch/order_book.hpp</span> vs <span class="path">cpp/itch/order_book_flat.hpp</span> —
the same public interface, two data structures. The baseline stores price levels in
<code>std::map</code> (red-black tree: pointer chasing, heap nodes, O(log&nbsp;n)). The flat book stores
them in a <code>std::vector&lt;uint32_t&gt;</code> indexed by
<code>(price − min_price) / tick_size</code> — O(1) updates, one contiguous 117&nbsp;KB allocation that
fits in L2 cache. The price range must be declared at construction: <strong>a domain constraint
(known tick range) buys a faster data structure.</strong></p>

<p><span class="path">docs/perf/01-flat-book.md</span> — the before/after evidence:
p50 <strong>83 ns → 42 ns (−49%)</strong>, p99 −21%, p99.9 −35%, with 15/15 tests still passing.
Read this write-up in full; it is the best single document in the repo about <em>why cache locality
is latency</em>.</p>

<p><span class="path">cpp/common/latency_histogram.hpp</span> — an HDR-style histogram
(power-of-2 buckets with sub-bucket resolution, 1 ns to ~68 s at ~1% precision). Recording a sample
is O(1) with no allocation, so the measurement doesn't distort the thing being measured.
<code>percentile(99.9)</code> is the headline number.</p>

<p><span class="path">cpp/itch/signal.hpp</span> — <code>make_signal()</code> turns either book's
top-of-book into <code>{bid, ask, mid, valid}</code>. The mid-price is the simplest possible
"fair value" estimate; microprice and order-flow imbalance are its future upgrades.</p>

