<h2 id="s5">5 · Often confused: HFT vs quantitative research</h2>

<p>People say "HFT" for anything algorithmic. This repo contains <strong>both</strong> disciplines,
and knowing which layer is which keeps your mental model honest:</p>

<div class="scrollbox">
<table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Quant research (this repo's Python layer)</th><th>HFT engineering (this repo's C++ layer)</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Question asked</strong></td><td>Does a predictive relationship exist? (CL leads XLE?)</td><td>Can we see and react to the market before competitors?</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Timescale</strong></td><td>Daily bars; lags measured in trading days</td><td>Nanoseconds; latency measured per message</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>What "fast" means</strong></td><td>Irrelevant — a 6-stage pipeline taking seconds is fine</td><td>Everything — 83 ns vs 42 ns p50 is a headline result</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Enemy</strong></td><td>Statistical artifacts: sign bugs, non-stationarity, look-ahead</td><td>Cache misses, allocation, tail-latency spikes</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Evidence artifact</strong></td><td>p-values, cointegration verdicts, CCF curves</td><td>Latency histogram percentiles, perf counters</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Shared ground</strong></td><td colspan="2">Both demand measurement before belief: parity tests, sign-convention tests, before/after benchmarks</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>Two more pairs worth keeping apart:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lead-lag correlation vs causation</strong> — <code>granger_both_directions()</code> exists precisely
  because x Granger-causing y only shows <em>predictive precedence</em>. The docstring says so.</li>
  <li><strong>Correlation (returns) vs cointegration (levels)</strong> — the pipeline runs CCF/Granger on
  <em>stationary returns</em> but Engle-Granger/Johansen on <em>price levels</em>. Cointegration asks
  whether a spread between prices is mean-reverting; correlation of returns can be high while prices drift apart forever.</li>
</ul>

