<h2 id="s7">7 · Check your understanding — scenarios</h2>

<details>
  <summary>Scenario A: You run the pipeline and it reports "leader: XLE" with peak lag −3. What's your first move?</summary>
  <p>Suspect a bug, not a discovery. The README says the expected stable-window result is CL leading
  XLE; the opposite "usually means a sign/alignment bug." Check series ordering into
  <code>ccf_leadlag(x, y)</code>, re-run the sign-convention unit test (<code>tests/test_leadlag.py</code>),
  and check whether your date window includes a distorted period. Only after those pass would you
  consider it a real regime change.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Scenario B: You want to trade a stock whose price can gap anywhere from $2 to $400 intraday. Which order book, and why?</summary>
  <p>The <code>std::map</code> baseline (<code>order_book.hpp</code>). The flat book needs declared price
  bounds at construction; a $2–$400 range at 0.0001 resolution would need ~4 million entries (16 MB —
  blowing out of cache and wasting the locality advantage), and any out-of-range print just increments
  <code>error_count</code>. The flat book wins when the tick range is <em>known and narrow</em> —
  the usual HFT case, but not this one.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Scenario C: Your Granger test says CL → XLE with p = 0.001. Can you say CL causes XLE moves?</summary>
  <p>No — only that CL returns contain information that <em>predicts</em> XLE returns beyond XLE's own
  history ("predictive precedence, not true causation," per the docstring). Both could be driven by a
  common factor (oil news) that hits the futures market's price first. That's also why
  <code>granger_both_directions()</code> always runs the reverse test.</p>
</details>

